	(text "For high-speed nanopore sensing, the answer is definitive: **Do NOT tie them together.**\n\nYou should use **Separate Voltage References** (or at least strictly buffered separate paths) for your ADC and your Bias Driver.\n\nHere is the engineering reality of why sharing a \"Reference Rail\" will introduce a mysterious noise floor that you can't filter out.\n\n### 1. The \"Bully\" on the Bus: The SAR ADC\n\nThe 16-bit ADC you are using (like the MCP33131) is a **SAR (Successive Approximation Register)** converter.\n\n* **The Physics:** During a conversion (1 million times a second), the ADC connects its internal capacitor array to the VREF pin to charge up.\n* **The Effect:** This draws massive, high-frequency current spikes (\"gulps\") from the reference pin.\n* **The Consequence:** If you look at an ADC reference pin with a fast scope, it looks like a saw-blade. It is vibrating with high-frequency noise.\n\n### 2. The \"Victim\": The Nanopore Bias\n\nYour Driver Circuit (the OPA192 circuit) takes the VREF voltage and applies it *directly* to the liquid in the nanopore chamber.\n\n* **The Consequence:** If you tie the Driver VREF to the ADC VREF, you are essentially taking that \"saw-blade\" noise from the ADC and injecting it straight into your nanopore.\n* **The Result:** Your TIA will measure that noise as \"signal.\" You will see 1 MHz spikes in your baseline data, and you won't be able to filter them out because they are technically \"real\" currents caused by your noisy bias.\n\n### 3. Drift vs. Noise (Why Isolation Wins)\n\nUsually, engineers tie references together to prevent \"Drift Error\" (so the DAC and ADC track each other as the room warms up).\n\n* **For Thermometers:** This matters.\n* **For Nanopores:** This **does not matter**.\n* *Why:* You are looking for rapid *changes* (DNA blocking the pore) that happen in microseconds. You don't care if the absolute baseline drifts from 180.0 mV to 180.1 mV over 10 minutes.\n* *Priority:* You care about **Noise**. You need the Bias to be \"dead silent\" in the 100 kHz band.\n\n\n\n### The Recommended Architecture\n\n**1. The ADC Reference (The \"Stiff\" One)**\n\n* **Chip:** **REF3440** (or similar 4.096V / 2.5V reference).\n* **Critical Feature:** Needs a massive **Bypass Capacitor** (10 µF to 47 µF) placed *immediately* at the ADC reference pin. This acts as a tank to supply the \"gulps\" of current without the voltage drooping.\n\n**2. The Bias Reference (The \"Quiet\" One)**\n\n* **Chip:** **REF3425** (or similar).\n* **Critical Feature:** Heavy **Low-Pass Filtering**.\n* Put a simple RC filter (e.g., ) between the VREF chip and your Op-Amp.\n* Since the Op-Amp input draws almost zero current, this resistor won't cause a voltage drop, but it will kill any residual silicon noise from the reference chip.\n\n\n\n### Summary\n\nSpend the extra $2.00 and put a separate Reference Chip on your Driver Board. It physically isolates your sensitive \"Source\" (the Bias) from your noisy \"Measurement\" (the ADC).\n\n**Next Step:** Would you like to check the capacitor selection for the ADC reference? If you pick the wrong ceramic type (e.g., Y5V vs X7R), the piezo effect can turn your board vibrations into voltage noise."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
